;; PROBE — the tf32 CONTROL for _probe_wgmma_bf16_scatter.  Not a ladder rung.
;;
;; WHY A CONTROL IS REQUIRED.  If the bf16 scatter probe verifies FALSE, that alone does not say
;; the fault is 16-bit-specific: the no-swizzle wgmma path has only ever been covered by
;; COMPILE-ONLY specs (140/00 checks forms and PTX, never a number), so it could be carrying a
;; pre-existing defect that nothing has executed.  This kernel is byte-for-byte the same shape at
;; tf32 -- K=8 instead of 16, float instead of bfloat16, nothing else.
;;
;;   tf32 TRUE  + bf16 FALSE -> the defect is in the 16-bit lowering.  Endeavour 160's problem.
;;   tf32 FALSE + bf16 FALSE -> the scatter wgmma path is broken for BOTH, predating this
;;                              endeavour entirely, and chapter 7's tf32 success comes from the
;;                              SWIZZLE path being the only one ever exercised numerically.
;;
;; The second outcome would be the more valuable finding, and it is the one no amount of staring
;; at the 16-bit diff could have produced.
;;
;; WHAT QUESTION THIS ANSWERS.  chap7_wgmma_bf16 measured verified=FALSE on an H100 PCIe while
;; the tf32 chapter 7 verified True on the same pod at the same size.  But chapter 7 changes FIVE
;; things at once relative to a plain kernel: TMA fetch, 128B swizzle, a 2-slot ring, warp
;; specialization, and wgmma itself.  A single failing bit over five simultaneous changes is not
;; a diagnosis.
;;
;; This kernel changes exactly ONE of them.  It is chapter 2's staging -- ordinary load-tile into
;; plain scratch, one warpgroup, no ring, no producer/consumer split, NO TMA, NO SWIZZLE -- with
;; the sync MMA replaced by wgmma.  So:
;;
;;   verified TRUE   -> the 16-bit wgmma lowering itself is sound (mnemonic, K-per-slice,
;;                      accumulator handling, the no-swizzle descriptor).  The fault in chapter 7
;;                      is then in the SWIZZLE/TMA staging, not in wgmma.
;;   verified FALSE  -> the fault is in the core 16-bit wgmma path, and chapter 7's machinery was
;;                      merely hiding it behind four other variables.
;;
;; EITHER ANSWER IS PROGRESS, which is the point of building it before renting again.
;;
;; NOTE THE DESCRIPTOR PATH DIFFERS FROM CHAPTER 7'S, deliberately.  Without :swizzle,
;; %wgmma-make-desc emits LBO=128B (enc 8) / SBO=256B (enc 16) -- entirely different constants
;; from the 128B-swizzle path's LBO=16B / SBO=1024B.  Reading CUTLASS's make_gmma_desc showed the
;; SWIZZLED constants should be byte-identical at bf16 and tf32 (LBO=1, SBO=64 in uint128 units
;; for both, given chapter 7's K-block choice), so the swizzled descriptor is probably NOT the
;; bug.  This probe exercises the other descriptor path and so tests a different part of the
;; claim.
;;
;; K IS 16 AND THERE IS NO :swizzle, so this is a single k16 slice -- the smallest possible unit
;; of 16-bit wgmma.  %emit-nvvm-wgmma's no-swizzle branch emits the proxy fence + barrier + ONE
;; instruction, so nothing about multi-slice advance (kk*32 bytes) is exercised either.  That is
;; another variable removed.
;;
;; Measured through benchmarks/matmul/crisp/bench_harness.cu, which verifies bf16 against its own
;; host reference.  The generated --mma-test harness CANNOT be used here: it fills A/B by writing
;; the small integers 0..4 as raw bit patterns (fp16 denormals) and reads them back as hardcoded
;; float, so its verdict for any 16-bit kernel is meaningless (endeavour 159).
;;
;; UNDERSCORE-PREFIXED so it is not mistaken for a ladder chapter and does not enter §1.5.

(def-type a-mat (matrix float :address-space :global :align :compact :contiguous-term :row-major))
(def-type b-mat (matrix float :address-space :global :align :compact :contiguous-term :col-major))
(def-type c-mat (matrix float    :address-space :global :align :compact :contiguous-term :row-major))

(def-kernel matmul (A B &out C)
  (declare #'(a-mat b-mat &out c-mat)
           (global-size :derive-from C :strategy :strided)
           (local-size :set-to 128))          ; ONE warpgroup, 128-aligned for wgmma
  (let ((A-tile (make-scratch-matrix float (64 8)))   ; M x K, a single k8 slice
        (B-tile (make-scratch-matrix float (64 8)))   ; N x K (B^T) -- wgmma reads B K-major
        (D      (make-wgmma-accumulator float (64 64) 0.0))
        (K      (inner-dimension A B)))
    (tile-stride C (64 64) (grid-y grid-x)
      (set! D (make-wgmma-accumulator float (64 64) 0.0))
      (dotimes (grid-k (/ (to-ulong K) (to-ulong 8)))
        (load-tile A A-tile (grid-y grid-k))
        (load-tile B B-tile (grid-k grid-x))
        (sync-workgroup)
        (wgmma-accumulate-via-tile (64 64 8) D A-tile B-tile)
        (sync-workgroup))
      :epilogue
      (store-tile D C (grid-y grid-x)))))
